<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burkhard Christoph von Münnich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1683">1683</a>–<a href="/years/1767">1767</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burkhard Christoph von Münnich was a German-born soldier and engineer who became a field marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He completed the Ladoga Canal, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1735-1739">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1735–1739 he stormed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perekop">Perekop</a> lines and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchysarai</a> in 1736, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ochakiv">Ochakov</a> and won at Stavuchany in 1739. Exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> by Empress Elizabeth in 1742, he was recalled by Peter III in 1762.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burkhard-christoph-von-munnich.jpg" alt="Burkhard Christoph von Münnich" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Buchholz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minikh_BK.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Burkhard Christoph von Münnich was born on 19 May 1683 at Neuenhuntorf in the County of Oldenburg, into a military family; his father Anton Günther was of East Frisian nobility. Besides his native Low German he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and French. Like his father he became an engineer and a specialist in hydrotechnology. He entered French service at 17 and moved on to the armies of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, where he reached the rank of colonel and later major general.</p><p>In 1721 the Russian ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, Alexey Grigoryevich Dolgorukov, invited him to engineering projects in the newly acquired northern territories. He presented Peter I with plans to fortify Kronstadt and the Annenkrone fortification in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Vyborg</a>, and was promoted to lieutenant general in 1722. He completed the costly Ladoga Canal, which had been under construction for over a decade, and in 1726 Catherine I made him general-in-chief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> from 1727 and from 1728 to 1734 was governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, receiving the title of count. After Anna's coronation in 1730 he became general field marshal and in 1732 president of the War Collegiate, charged with reorganising the army. He founded the Izmaylovsky Regiment of the Imperial Guard and the Shlyakhetskiy Cadet Corps, created the first hussar regiments and sapper regiments, set up an engineer school for officers and had some fifty fortresses built. In 1734 he was sent to take Danzig, a siege for which he was heavily reproached.</p><p>As commander in the Turkish campaigns he stormed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perekop">Or Qapı</a> fortress, entered the Crimean Peninsula and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> cities of Kezlev, Aqmescit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchisaray</a>, but poor logistics and the exhaustion of his troops forced him out. On 2 July 1737 he took Özi (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ochakiv">Ochakov</a>) with the help of the artillery. In 1739 he won the Battle of Stavuchany on 28 August, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khotyn">Khotyn</a> on 30 August and entered Iași on 14 September, forcing the Moldavian boyars to accept annexation. The Treaty of Niš of 3 October returned almost all the conquests, and he received the Order of St Andrew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His rivalry with Biron, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, whom he had arrested, led to his own arrest under Elizabeth. He was condemned to death, withdrawn from the scaffold and sent to Pelym in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, where he stayed twenty years until Peter III released him in 1762. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> made him director-general of the Baltic ports. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> on 27 October 1767 and was buried on his estate near it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> called him the Prince Eugene of the Muscovites, and his aide-de-camp Christoph Hermann von Manstein described a man of both generosity and greed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burkhard Christoph von Münnich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1736, during the Russo-Turkish War, a Russian army under <b>Burkhard Christoph von Münnich</b> stormed Perekop and burned Bakhchysarai, and slave raiding declined as Russian and Polish frontiers advanced.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Berne, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.18,8.48&amp;year=1737" aria-label="Berne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Burkhard Christoph von Münnich"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5433 5554h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5433 5554h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.18,8.48&amp;year=1737">What was here in 1737? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
